  "review": {
    "kind": "structure",
    "description": "Interaction S1-S5 preserve immediate chat, steer, native blocking requests and explicit interruption holds; cache eviction does not replay a command or create a replacement thread.",
    "implementation": "The diff does not change session control, native RPC delivery or queue flushing. The new store inventory reads metadata in its caller transaction and only cache rows are replaced/deleted by its provider caller. Eviction inventories only CheckedAt and Expires alongside authoritative storage IDs and decoded body sizes. Unselected bodies, Header, ETag and Redirect payloads remain in SQLite. Field names, byte path and kind are bound values; cursor closure permits subsequent mutation within the same transaction. The durable response itself remains unchanged, and in-memory changes follow durable commit.",
    "tests": "Read TestChatFlushBusySteerHoldAndRetainedReuse: chat bypasses quiet time, automatic batching leaves an active turn alone, steer drains pending events, interrupt holds the queue and resume reuses the sole thread. Cache rollback tests validate separate provider persistence failure handling. TestJSONByteEntriesKeepBodylessMetadataAndTransactionalIdentity seeds five rows with 128 KiB each of Link, ETag and Redirect data; selected CheckedAt/ID metadata stays within 128 bytes and two fields per row. It checks authoritative IDs/decoded sizes, bound bucket/path/field inputs, empty selection and unchanged persisted body/ETag after mutation. TestGitHubPersistentCacheEvictionKeepsLargeHeadersAfterRestart seeds two 1 MiB Link responses, retires the 25 hour old row and preserves the fresh exact Link/body through restart. Both use temporary SQLite state; the provider fixture has a 10 second failure bound and deferred cleanup."
  },
